00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.069 align:start position:0%
 
we've talked quite a bit about  intelligence on this program  some examples are we've had discussions  about cognitive abilities we've talked  about behavioral economics we've talked  about the dunning-kruger phenomenon to  the extent that it's valid I known it  that now there are some recent questions  about to what extent is it valid we've  spoken about moral psychology we've  talked about the right-wing effort to  smear higher education as liberal  indoctrination I've spoken a lot about  pseudo versus anti intellectualism we've  also talked about education in the  aggregate in the United States where we  see rising education on average right  people are more educated than ever more  college degrees but there's a sort of  ugly aspect in terms of that growing  college educated population which is the  growing intellectual divide the sort of  inequality in education and the  cognitive divide that is growing and  that's what I want to talk about today  Nick medjool II wrote a really good  article about this on his blog called  of dollars and data and it's mostly like  a wonky personal finance website but he  has a really good article called the  cognitive aristocracy and it was  recommended to me by Barry Ritholtz 'iz  newsletter which I encourage you to get  on if you don't know who Barry Ritholtz  is google him get on his newsletter  really really good reading  recommendations there um and what Nick  Manjula points out is that we have a  situation where on the one hand in the  United States we have more people with  college degrees than ever as a  percentage of the population on the  other hand we have academic achievement  by American students in math and science  and reading really lagging behind other  countries and not improving either  falling further and further behind many  other countries so let's put aside for a  second the questions about the value of  standardized testing as a way to measure  this because I agree standardized  testing is not a great measure of total  cognitive  but for today we can consider it sort of  a proxy to think about what's going on  what we have is a status quo where  Americans are more educated than ever in  total but are lagging more and more in  actual academic achievement and one of  the reasons for this is that over time  the people who are most likely to go to  college are coming more and more from  those with the most cognitive ability  okay you can see that since the early  1900's people at all cognitive levels  are going to college more but at the top  people in the 90th percentile of  cognitive ability people in the 75th  percentile of cognitive ability their  college attendance has increased by  significantly more than those who don't  do as well on cognitive tests now all  sorts of things are said about IQ many  of those things are controversial and  many of those things we've debunked  because they're flat-out wrong but as  I'm going to point out in the next  couple of weeks in a follow-up on the  race and IQ topic there is something  that IQ does really well which is it  screens out people who are seriously  cognitively limited so what we're  learning more and more is that people  who have IQs of 150 don't really do much  better in life than people who have IQs  of 120 or 105 but that when IQ is  particularly low which really just tells  us that certain cognitive abilities are  really lacking that is a significant  negative indicator in terms of future  success so we can debate whether college  is the path forward for career success  the answer is in our economy it's  generally helpful but in the new economy  it might be more circumstance dependent  but the impact of all of this is that  the workforce is increasingly divided  into two groups well educated  professionals are seeing rising wages  most workers are in stagnant wage jobs  with little opportunity  for advancement and this is correlating  with increasingly having two groups in  terms of intellect as well and the sort  of downside one of the downsides of our  system is that we often fail to identify  poor and minority students who could do  really well with higher education but  they're simply not identified as having  that potential and then they suffer and  in principle there's no problem simply  because different people have different  education levels you would expect in a  world where equality of outcome is not  realistically to be expected that you're  going to end up with people with  different educational levels the problem  is that education is correlating with  income and income correlates with all  sorts of divides in society from a  cultural perspective the biggest problem  is that ignorant and lack of education  are increasingly seeing as a badge of  honor in some circles we've talked about  how on the Republican side there is more  and more demonization of higher  education as liberal indoctrination in  the short term this helps Republicans  get elected because you have people who  are proud of not being educated or of  being anti intellectuals and they're  overwhelmingly voting for Republicans so  in the short term this actually has a  political consequence of helping  Republicans in the long term though this  could destroy our country as we know it  by creating a sort of even more  permanent underclass and I say even more  permanent because class mobility is  difficult enough as it is if you're born  poor you're overwhelmingly likely to  remain poor for the rest of your life  it's going to be even more difficult if  this correlation between education  intellect cognitive achievement and  income continues to grow and the sort of  partisan analysis is yeah a lot of these  people just write off education is  liberal nonsense too bad for them  that's fine from a political perspective  but this goes way beyond politics in  that it affects people way more on the  basis of economic class  on the basis of politics it affects  people way more on the basis of minority  status than on the basis of their  politics and there is zero chance that  Republicans fix it  between gutting public education and  seeing college as liberal nonsense and  pushing charter schools at the expense  of public schools Republicans I mean  there is zero chance they are going to  fix it it's something that has to change  and to play politics with it is fine it  doesn't help the left though because of  the issue that educated people tend to  vote more liberally but it's a far  bigger structural problem a sort of  cognitive and intellectual underclass  that is rapidly developing in the United  States of America

